## Feature Design Guidance

### Design for scale and governance

MailPro is for advanced email operations, not lightweight newsletter sending only.

Good designs should support:

- Multiple brands
- Multiple teams
- Large databases
- Approval workflows
- Role-based permissions
- Central control
- Reusable content
- Advanced data structures

Avoid:

- Assuming one small team manages everything
- Hiding governance logic
- Treating approvals as optional comments outside the system

---

### Make reusable content central

Reusable templates, modules, content types, and assets are key to MailPro’s value.

Good designs should make it easy to:

- Find reusable content
- Understand where content is used
- Update content centrally
- Reuse modules across campaigns
- Maintain brand consistency

Avoid making every email feel like a one-off design.

---

### Preserve relational data logic

MailPro’s data model is more advanced than flat lists.

Good designs should show:

- Collections
- Linked datasets
- Contact relationships
- Behaviour and preferences
- Filters based on connected data
- Data sources used in personalisation

Avoid simplifying all targeting to static contact lists.

---

### Keep automation visual and editable

The visual flow builder should make automation accessible.

Good designs should show:

- Trigger
- Steps
- Conditions
- Branches
- Timing
- Status
- Live flow update state

Avoid technical workflow language unless the feature is specifically for technical users.

---

### Make approvals and permissions visible

Governance should be clear at the moment it matters.

Good designs should show:

- Who can edit
- Who can approve
- Who can send
- What approval is required
- Current approval status
- Required changes
- Permission limitations

Avoid allowing users to progress without understanding approval requirements.

---

### Make reports shareable and actionable

MailPro reporting should help teams communicate results and improve campaigns.

Good designs should support:

- Campaign summaries
- Performance summaries
- Exportable reports
- Contact trends
- Campaign trends
- A/B test outcomes
- Stakeholder sharing

Avoid dashboards that only show metrics without interpretation or exportability.

---

## Important Assumptions to Preserve

Preserve these assumptions when generating product concepts, UX flows, screens, or feature ideas:

- Spotler MailPro is an advanced email marketing platform.
- It is built for complex structures, large databases, multiple brands, business units, approval needs, and advanced data.
- It is not just a basic newsletter tool.
- Reusable templates, assets, modules, and content types are central.
- Relational databases and collections are key product concepts.
- Targeting and personalisation can use multiple linked datasets per contact.
- Visual automation flows support simple and advanced journeys.
- Live automation flows can be updated, but this should feel controlled.
- Governance is a core product value.
- Role-based access and approval workflows matter.
- Reporting should be clear, shareable, and exportable.
- A/B testing supports subject line and content optimisation.
- Integrations depend on the organisation’s data model and setup.
- Spotler Message can extend campaigns with WhatsApp.
- Spotler Activate can enrich targeting with behavioural data and audience segmentation.

---

## Things Figma Make Should Avoid

Avoid these mistakes when designing Spotler MailPro features:

- Do not treat MailPro as a basic newsletter tool.
- Do not treat MailPro as the same product as Mail+.
- Do not assume one brand, one team, or one template set.
- Do not ignore role-based access, approvals, and governance.
- Do not design sending flows that bypass approval logic.
- Do not reduce targeting to flat lists when relational data is relevant.
- Do not hide collections or linked datasets in data-driven features.
- Do not make automation feel like developer-only workflow programming.
- Do not ignore exportable reporting and stakeholder sharing.
- Do not invent fixed integrations that depend on setup unless the feature context provides them.
- Do not treat WhatsApp as native MailPro functionality without the Spotler Message integration.
- Do not replace product terminology with vague terms like “items”, “records”, or “objects”.
- Do not overfocus on visual styling; that belongs in the Design System Skill.

---

## Edge Cases and Product Logic to Consider

### User lacks permission

A user may not be allowed to edit, approve, configure, or send. Designs should show permission limitations clearly.

### Campaign requires approval

A campaign may be complete but cannot be sent until approval is granted.

### Approval rejected

A reviewer may request changes. Designs should support feedback, revision, and resubmission.

### Multiple brands share a platform

Templates, assets, permissions, and campaigns may need brand-specific filtering or access.

### Reusable module updated centrally

A content module may be used in multiple campaigns. Updating it may affect several places, so designs should show impact where relevant.

### Relational data incomplete

A campaign may use linked datasets that are missing for some contacts. Designs should support fallback content or clear warnings.

### Filter returns too few contacts

Advanced filters may create very small or empty target groups. Designs should help users understand why.

### Live automation flow update

A flow may already be active. Changes should be clear, controlled, and safe.

### Integration depends on setup

Data integrations may differ per customer. Designs should not assume one fixed schema or connector.

### Report needs to be shared externally

Stakeholders may need exported reports. Designs should support export and clear summaries.

### A/B test volume too low

A test may not have enough volume to produce a meaningful result. Designs should avoid overstating certainty.

---

## Relationship to the Separate Design System Skill

This Skill provides product context only.

Use this Skill for:

- Product behaviour
- Workflows
- MailPro terminology
- User roles
- Content management logic
- Template and asset management logic
- Relational data and collection logic
- Personalisation and filter logic
- Automation flow logic
- Approval and permission logic
- Reporting and optimisation context
- Integration context
- Feature-specific product assumptions

Do not use this Skill for:

- Component styling
- Typography
- Colour usage
- Layout systems
- Spacing
- Icons
- Button styles
- Form styling
- Navigation visual patterns
- Design tokens

For those topics, use the separate Spotler Design System Skill or attached Design System documentation.

When both Skills are available:

- Use this Skill to decide what the feature should do and how it should behave.
- Use the Design System Skill to decide how the feature should look and be constructed visually.

---

## Final Checklist for Figma Make

Before generating or redesigning a Spotler MailPro feature, check:

- Does the design use MailPro terminology?
- Is the relevant user role clear?
- Is the feature grounded in advanced email marketing, templates, modules, relational data, automation, governance, reporting, or integrations?
- Does the design avoid basic newsletter-tool assumptions?
- Does it account for multi-brand or multi-business-unit complexity where relevant?
- Does it preserve role-based access and approval workflow logic?
- Does it make reusable content central?
- Does it show relational data, collections, linked datasets, or filters where relevant?
- Does it make automation triggers, steps, conditions, timing, and live status clear?
- Does reporting support sharing and optimisation?
- Does it avoid inventing setup-dependent integrations?
- Does it avoid visual styling decisions that belong to the Design System Skill?

